  completeExplanation: 'Thermal science rests on the foundational distinction between heat and temperature. Temperature quantifies the average thermal kinetic state of particles (measured on the Celsius scale or SI unit kelvin K), whereas heat is thermal energy spontaneously transferred across a temperature gradient from higher to lower temperature regions. Heat transfer proceeds via three distinct physical mechanisms: Conduction occurs primarily in solids, where kinetic energy propagates through lattice vibrations and free electron collisions from atom to neighbour without any bulk displacement of matter. Metals with abundant free electrons are superior conductors (ideal for cookware), whereas materials with immobilised electrons (wood, glass, porcelain, plastic) and still air are poor conductors (insulators). Trapped air is an outstanding natural insulator exploited in woollen knitwear, dual-blanket layering, hollow-brick masonry, and traditional Himalayan mud-wood architecture (Uttarkashi). Convection occurs exclusively in fluids (liquids and gases) where mobile particles upon heating expand, decrease in density, and rise under buoyant force, while cooler, denser fluid descends to replace them, establishing continuous convection currents. In nature, differential heating between land and sea drives coastal convection: during daylight, low specific heat land heats rapidly, creating rising air and inducing cool "sea breezes" from water to shore; at night, land cools faster than high heat capacity seawater, creating "land breezes" blowing offshore. Radiation transfers thermal energy across vacuum via electromagnetic infrared waves without requiring any physical medium-explaining how solar radiation traverses 150 million km of interplanetary space. Surface optical properties govern radiative interaction: white/light surfaces reflect infrared radiation (cooling summer attire), whereas dark matte surfaces absorb radiation efficiently (warming winter attire). In practical engineering (such as boiling water or the Himalayan Bukhari stove), conduction, convection, and radiation operate concurrently. In the biosphere, solar radiation powers the hydrological cycle, driving evaporation and plant transpiration into the atmosphere, where ascending vapour condenses into clouds and precipitates as rain or snow. Terrestrial water infiltrates porous geological strata-permeating gravel rapidly and clay slowly-recharging subterranean aquifers. Sustainable human interventions include rainwater harvesting and Ladakhi Ice Stupas, conical artificial glaciers engineered to minimize solar surface area and release fresh meltwater during spring crop sowing.',
